2016 is an important year for the BMW  group and March had celebrated its 100th  birthday to mark the centenary the BMW  Museum in Munich has put on a temporary  exhibition presenting the company's  history as exemplified in 100  masterpieces that testified to the  innovation and enterprise displayed in  various epochs  the company started out making aeroplane  engines in 1916 seven years later it  started making motorcycles in 1928 BMWs  first car made its debut the earliest  models were small and contemporary in  design at the Berlin Motor Show in 1933  BMW presented the 303  it's sported a new six-cylinder in-line  engine the interior was spacious the  saloon represented a shift from compact  to mid-range models the 303 was the  first car to sport the kidney grille  which would become a typical BMW feature  it had a ladder frame made from tubular  side members and box section cross  members which meant the car was  unusually light at the time the  consensus was that only heavy vehicles  had good road-holding  decks or not the director of the museum  gabriella think tells us that the  exhibits come from all over some were  donated by members of the BMW clubs many  others were very generously made  available by friends at various  exhibition halls and museums after the  Second World War the company had to  reinvent itself in many respects it only  resumed making cars in 1952 but it was  not very successful the breakthrough  came with the introduction of the 1500  unveiled in 1961 as the first model of  what BMW called its new class it was an  eye catcher with its modern dynamic  styling  the new class as a whole proved a huge  success with more than 350,000 units  sold by 1972  new class vehicles also took part in  races and often won an old advertisement  says Touring Car races are to this day  BMWs domain the list of BMW successes  keeps getting longer  Robert Hana won the European Touring Car  Championship in 1966 he was the first to  zip around the Nurburgring track in less  than 10 minutes at a touring car between  1919 and 1970 BMW won 321 World Records  373 championships and six thousand two  hundred eighty seven gold medals and  book nine thousand seven hundred sixteen  wins in various classes the four-door  sedan version of the 1500 with a new  four-cylinder 80 horsepower engine was  fast and sporting by 1971 an entire  model range had been created with a top  model packing 130 horsepower  experience on the racetrack is useful  for everyday driving - validated and  racing and reliability tests  and in everyday deriving me envy  in 1999 the BMW v12 LMR one philemon  Grand Prix Pierluigi martini drove the  580 horsepower beast in the grueling 24  hour race 366 times around the circuit  of 13 point 6 kilometers  it was the first Lomond victory for BMW  these are just a few of the exciting  stories and fabulous vehicles that can  be enjoyed at the 100 masterpieces  exhibition at the BMW Museum in Munich
 

